//! member: true
//!
//! std.env — the program's arguments and the host's environment
//! variables. Stability tier: core.
//!
//! **This is the module that lets a project stop holding its input as
//! data.** Until argv arrives, every program in a corpus is a literal
//! with a `main` around it; `args()` is the seam where a real one starts.
//!
//! **Capability: `env` (I13).** Every function here except the two pure
//! entry helpers reaches the host's process context, is tagged `env` in
//! the toolchain's sandbox table, and is categorically refused at
//! comptime (D33) for the reason the table gives: *determinism and
//! confinement — environment contents differ per machine and may hold
//! secrets*. The refusal is `E0701`; `tests/env/comptime_refuses.lu`
//! holds it one level down at the builtin, because a `comptime fn` cannot
//! call across a module boundary at this pin (F-0051).
//!
//! `entry_key` and `entry_value` are PURE and comptime-safe — they cut a
//! `K=V` line and touch nothing. §14 says to state that exception
//! explicitly, because in a capability module the pure function is the
//! surprising one.
//!
//! **Errors are rows and the vocabulary is the toolchain's, verbatim**
//! (§14): `missing` for a variable the host does not have, `utf8` for a
//! value the host holds in an encoding this machine cannot decode, and
//! `invalid` for a NAME the platform cannot store. std adds no
//! translation layer, no error type of its own, and no coarsening a
//! caller did not ask for. Nothing here traps: a host that does not have
//! a variable is not a caller contract violation.
//!
//! `missing` joins §2's absence family — "there is nothing here" — while
//! `invalid` is an error mark: it names an argument the CALLER got wrong
//! (an empty name, or one containing `=` or NUL), and it is a row rather
//! than a trap only because the platforms disagree about which names they
//! will hold, so a caller cannot portably check in advance.
//!
//! **The arguments are the program's, not the runtime's.** `args()` is
//! what the user typed after the program name — it does not include the
//! executable path, and it does not include the toolchain's own flags.
//! Under `wolf conform-run` and `lupin conform-run` there are NO
//! arguments on any lane, by design (the checked machine mirrors the
//! stdin posture and the native rung runs the binary bare), so
//! `args().len` is 0 in every test in this repository and in every doc
//! example below. `wolf run file.lu a b c` is where real arguments
//! arrive. A test that asserted a specific argument would be asserting
//! the rig, not the language.
//!
//! **`set` writes, and what it writes to is not the same place on every
//! lane.** The checked machine keeps a machine-local overlay; the native
//! rung writes the real process environment. The same program observes
//! the same answers either way — which is exactly what makes a round
//! trip (`set` then `get`) the honest test — but a value set here is not
//! visible to a child process on the checked lane, and `std.process`
//! does not exist yet to notice.
//!
//! **Lanes.** The checked tier and the native rung both execute the
//! `env_*` builtins; lupin 0.1.6 has none of them and refuses at resolve.
//! It resolves module bodies LAZILY, so a program that touches only
//! `entry_key`/`entry_value` runs there — which is why
//! `tests/env/entry_helpers.lu` has three lanes and the rest have two.
//!
//! Reviewed contracts, blocked or unscheduled:
//!
//! ```text
//! unset(name: str) -> () ! {invalid}
//!     Remove a variable. There is no `env_unset` builtin: `set(name,
//!     "")` is NOT the same operation (an empty value is a value, and
//!     `has` answers true for it), so std ships nothing rather than an
//!     approximation that silently means something else.
//! pairs() -> List[(str, str)]
//!     `vars()` already split. Blocked by the language: `List[(str,
//!     str)]` is `unsupported` in wolfc (F-0026's tuple-element gap),
//!     which is why the split ships as two pure functions over one line
//!     instead of as a shape.
//! program() -> str
//!     The executable's own path. Not in the builtin tier — `env_args`
//!     is the arguments only — and inventing it from `args()` would
//!     hand back the first argument, which is a different thing.
//! cwd() / chdir()
//!     The working directory. `os_cwd` exists at the builtin tier and
//!     carries this same `env` capability. sc10 expected it to land in a
//!     `std.os` alongside `os_exit` and the process trio; sc11 shipped
//!     `std.process` instead — the trio and `os_exit` (as
//!     `process.exit`) live there, because "process" is the noun those
//!     three operations share and `std.os` would have been a module named
//!     after a layer rather than a concept (§1: modules are nouns).
//!     `os_cwd` is the one member of the s40 os family with no home yet:
//!     it is `env`-tagged rather than `exec`-tagged, it is a QUERY about
//!     this program's situation exactly as `args` and `vars` are, and the
//!     honest reason it is not in this module is that `chdir` has no
//!     builtin at all — so a `cwd` here would be a read with no write
//!     beside it, which is a shape to decide deliberately rather than by
//!     accident. Filed as F-0067 with the missing half named.
//! ```

/// The program's arguments, in the order the user gave them.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps. Excludes the executable's own path (see the
/// module header). The list is freshly allocated in the ambient region on
/// every call; nothing here is cached, because a cache would be a
/// promise the host has not made.
///
/// Capability `env`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `env_args`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// env.args().len >= 0
/// ```
pub fn args() -> List[str] {
    env_args()
}

/// The argument at `i`, counting from zero.
///
/// Rows: `none` when `i` is negative or past the last argument — an
/// absence and not a trap, because an index into argv is DATA the program
/// received rather than a bound the caller could have checked (§8's rule,
/// in the place it matters most: the index usually comes from the
/// program's own option parser).
///
/// Capability `env`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `env_args`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// (env.arg(0) else "<none>") == "<none>"
/// (env.arg(-1) else "<none>") == "<none>"
/// ```
pub fn arg(i: int) -> str ! {none} {
    let all = env_args()
    if i < 0 {
        return none
    }
    if i >= all.len {
        return none
    }
    all[i]
}

/// The value of the environment variable `name`.
///
/// Rows: `missing` when the host has no such variable, `utf8` when it has
/// one whose value is not well-formed UTF-8 (a wolf `str` is UTF-8 by
/// definition, so a value that cannot decode is a miss and never a lossy
/// read). Never traps.
///
/// An empty value is a VALUE: `get` of a variable set to `""` answers
/// `""` and not `missing`. That distinction is the whole reason this
/// returns a row instead of a string.
///
/// Bare `get` is the checked-access spelling and this one can miss (§1),
/// so the name is right and the row is not decoration.
///
/// Capability `env`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `env_get`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// env.set("WOLF_STD_DOC_GET", "wolf")?
/// (env.get("WOLF_STD_DOC_GET") else "?") == "wolf"
/// (env.get("WOLF_STD_DOC_ABSENT") else "?") == "?"
/// ```
pub fn get(name: str) -> str ! {missing, utf8} {
    env_get(name)?
}

/// Whether `get(name)` would answer with a value.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps. `false` for a variable the host does not
/// have AND for one whose value this machine cannot decode — the two are
/// deliberately one answer, because the question this predicate asks is
/// "can I read it", and a caller who needs to tell the cases apart calls
/// `get` and handles the tags.
///
/// Capability `env`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `env_get`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// env.set("WOLF_STD_DOC_HAS", "1")?
/// env.has("WOLF_STD_DOC_HAS") == true
/// env.has("WOLF_STD_DOC_NOT_SET") == false
/// ```
pub fn has(name: str) -> bool {
    let probe = env_get(name) else |_| { return false }
    probe.len >= 0
}

/// Every environment variable the host has, as `K=V` lines sorted by
/// name.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps. The sort is the builtin tier's and it is a
/// DETERMINISM promise, not a convenience: two runs of one program see
/// the same order, and a program that prints its environment produces a
/// diffable output. Cut a line with `entry_key` and `entry_value`.
///
/// A variable whose value the host holds in a non-UTF-8 encoding is
/// omitted rather than mangled — the same posture `get`'s `utf8` row
/// takes, in a function that has no row to say it with.
///
/// Capability `env`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `env_vars`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// env.set("WOLF_STD_DOC_VARS", "x")?
/// env.vars().len > 0
/// ```
pub fn vars() -> List[str] {
    env_vars()
}

/// Set the environment variable `name` to `value` for this process.
///
/// Rows: `invalid` when the platform cannot hold the name — empty, or
/// containing `=` or a NUL. Never traps: which names a platform accepts
/// differs between hosts, so a caller could not portably have checked
/// (§2's trap rule).
///
/// Setting is process-global and is not undone at any scope exit; a test
/// that sets a variable is writing to the program it runs in. Use names
/// nobody else will (`WOLF_STD_…`), which is what this repository's own
/// tests and examples do.
///
/// Capability `env`; refused at comptime (D33). Delegates to `env_set`.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// env.set("WOLF_STD_DOC_SET", "value")?
/// (env.get("WOLF_STD_DOC_SET") else "?") == "value"
/// ```
pub fn set(name: str, value: str) -> () ! {invalid} {
    env_set(name, value)?
}

/// The name half of a `K=V` line from `vars()`.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps, and PURE — comptime-safe, reaching no host.
/// A line with no `=` at all is all name and no value, which is what a
/// caller wants for the degenerate case: `entry_key("BARE")` is
/// `"BARE"`.
///
/// The cut is at the FIRST `=`, because a value may contain one and a
/// name may not (that is exactly what `set`'s `invalid` row is about).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// env.entry_key("HOME=/home/wolf") == "HOME"
/// env.entry_key("EMPTY=") == "EMPTY"
/// env.entry_key("BARE") == "BARE"
/// env.entry_key("A=b=c") == "A"
/// ```
pub fn entry_key(entry: str) -> str {
    let cut = entry.find("=") else -1
    if cut < 0 {
        return entry
    }
    entry[0..cut]
}

/// The value half of a `K=V` line from `vars()`.
///
/// Total: no rows, no traps, and PURE — comptime-safe, reaching no host.
/// A line with no `=` has an empty value. Everything after the first `=`
/// is the value, `=` signs included.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// env.entry_value("HOME=/home/wolf") == "/home/wolf"
/// env.entry_value("EMPTY=") == ""
/// env.entry_value("BARE") == ""
/// env.entry_value("A=b=c") == "b=c"
/// ```
pub fn entry_value(entry: str) -> str {
    let cut = entry.find("=") else -1
    if cut < 0 {
        return ""
    }
    entry[cut + 1..]
}
